    "discipline": "Environmental",
    "subject": "Environmental Chemistry",
    "chapter": "Multimedia Equilibrium Partitioning",
    "topic": "Henry's Law",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<p>The mass of a gas that dissolves in given mass of a solvent at a constant temperature is directly proportional to the pressure of the gas at equilibrium with the solution provided.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>This law is applicable to ideal gases only. However this law can be applied to real gases, especially at low pressure, where the behavior of real gas approached ideal gases.</li>\r\n<li>This law is not applicable to those gases which undergo a chemical change in the situation, such as Ammonia becomes Ammonium Hydroxide.</li>\r\n<li>This law cannot be applied to those gases which dissolve into the ions in the solution.</li>\r\n</ul>",
