    "discipline": "Environmental",
    "subject": "Surface Water Resources and Hydrology",
    "chapter": "Water Quality and Modeling",
    "topic": "Wetlands",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class= \"container-fluid\">\r\n  <div class= \"row align-items-start\">\r\n    <div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n   <p>Wetlands are transitional areas between terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems where water is present at or near the surface, either permanently or seasonally.</p>\r\n\r\nTypes:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Marshes: Shallow wetlands dominated by grasses and herbaceous plants.</li>\r\n<li>Swamps: Wetlands dominated by trees and woody vegetation.</li>\r\n<li>Bogs: Acidic wetlands characterized by peat accumulation and often dominated by mosses.</li>\r\n<li>Estuarine Wetlands: Found in coastal areas, influenced by both saltwater and freshwater.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\nFunctions and Services\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Water Filtration: Wetlands act as natural filters, trapping sediments and pollutants, improving water quality.</li>\r\n<li>Flood Control: Absorb excess water during storms, reducing the risk of flooding downstream.</li>\r\n<li>Habitat Provision: Support diverse plant and animal species, serving as crucial breeding and feeding grounds.</li>\r\n<li>Biodiversity: Rich biodiversity due to varied microenvironments and nutrient cycling.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n    </div>\r\n    <div class=\"col\">\r\n <center><p><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/USGS_image_cropped.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"></p><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USGS_image_cropped.jpg\">Douglas A. Wilcox, Todd A. Thompson, Robert K. Booth, and J.R. Nicholas</a>, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>",
