    "discipline": "Environmental",
    "subject": "Water and Wastewater",
    "chapter": "Biological Processes",
    "topic": "Activated Sludge",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"container-fluid\">\r\n\t<div class=\"row align-items-start\">\r\n\t\t<div class=\"col col-lg-6\">\r\n\r\nActivated Sludge Process Most  commonly used secondary treatment process \r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Microbes, mainly aerobic heterotrophic bacteria, are involved Designed to remove (soluble) biodegradable organic matter </li>\r\n<li>Removal of nutrients, TSS, pathogens and heavy metals is coincidental Usually clarified sewage (primary effluents) is treated </li>\r\n<li>Primary treatment is omitted in case of small flows and low TSS sewage, and in hot climates (to avoid/control odour problems) </li>\r\n<li>SBR, oxidation ditches, aerated lagoons, contact-stabilization process, etc. may not require primary treatment Treatment involves conversion of soluble organic matter into biological flocs and their removal as secondary sludge Includes an aeration tank and a secondary sedimentation tank </li>\r\n<li>Aeration and mixing, and sludge recycling are additional features</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\t\t</div>\r\n    \t\t<div class=\"col\">\r\n     <center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Activated_Sludge_2.png\" alt=\"Activated Sludge\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Activated_Sludge_2.png\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Kekelhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:MbeychokUser:LennyCZ</a>, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    \t\t</div>\r\n  \t</div>\r\n</div>",
    "important": "",
    "resources": "",
    "images": [],
    "local_images": [],
    "video": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1lvIIDpGCWer6nGgKJdZk0hN2rETnP3bM/preview",
    "videoTitle": "Activated Sludge"
