    "discipline": "Environmental",
    "subject": "Water and Wastewater",
    "chapter": "Chemical Processes",
    "topic": "Coagulation",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class= \"container-fluid\">\r\n  <div class= \"row align-items-start\">\r\n   <div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n   <p>Coagulation: The process of destabilizing and clumping together small particles in water to form larger, settleable flocs.</p>\r\n\r\nCommon Coagulants:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Aluminum Sulfate (Alum): Commonly used coagulant.</li>\r\n<li>Ferric Sulfate: Effective in treating both surface water and wastewater.</li>\r\n<li>Polyaluminum Chloride (PAC): Provides improved coagulation performance.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n    </div>\r\n    <div class=\"col\">\r\n<center><p><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/The_coagulation_and_filtration_processes_at_a_drinking_water_treatment_plant._%2814868618507%29.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"></p><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_coagulation_and_filtration_processes_at_a_drinking_water_treatment_plant._(14868618507).jpg\">USEPA Environmental-Protection-Agency</a>, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>",
