    "topic": "Trigonometry",
    "subtopic": "Basic Trigonometric Definitions",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"notes-image-container my-4 text-center flex flex-col items-center\"><center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Right_triangle_with_trigonometric_variables_x%2C_y%2C_r%2C_%CE%B8%2C_August_2019.svg\" alt=\"Trigonometry triangle\" width=\"340\"><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Right_triangle_with_trigonometric_variables_x,_y,_r,_%CE%B8,_August_2019.svg\">Steven Baltakatei Sandoval</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/></div><p>The three basic trigonometric ratios are defined as follows:</p>\r\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\cos\\ \\theta =\r\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Adjacent}}{\\mathrm{Hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{x}{r}\\]</span>\r\n<span class=\"math display\">\\[\\sin\\ \\theta =\r\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Opposite}}{\\mathrm{Hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{y}{r}\\]</span>\r\n<span class=\"math display\">\\[\\tan\\ \\theta =\r\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Opposite}}{\\mathrm{Adjacent}} = \\dfrac{x}{y}\\]</span></p>\r\n<p>The remaining trigonometric functions can be defined as reciprocals:\r\n\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$\\begin{align*}\r\n\\cot\\ \\theta &= \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\ \\theta}\\\\\r\n\\sec\\ \\theta &= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\ \\theta}\\\\\r\n\\csc\\ \\theta &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\ \\theta}\r\n\\end{align*}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 38</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>",
    "important": "",
    "resources": "",
    "images": [
      "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Right_triangle_with_trigonometric_variables_x%2C_y%2C_r%2C_%CE%B8%2C_August_2019.svg"
    ],
    "local_images": []
