    "discipline": "Environmental",
    "subject": "Fluid Mechanics and Hydraulics",
    "chapter": "Fluid Properties",
    "topic": "Surface Tension and Capillarity",
    "subtopic": "Capillarity",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"notes-image-container my-4 text-center flex flex-col items-center\"><center><img src=\"https://onedrive.live.com/embed?resid=9C913AE725AFDEB1%21581&authkey=%21AOQoVTXCapx5-0Y&width=323&height=292\" width=\"323\" height=\"292\" /></center>\r\n<center><img src=\"https://onedrive.live.com/embed?resid=9C913AE725AFDEB1%21570&authkey=%21APWq79faLJnDMCo&width=323&height=292\" width=\"323\" height=\"292\" /></center></div>The capillary rise h is approximated by</p>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$h = \\dfrac{4\\sigma \\cos \\beta}{\\gamma d}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 178</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\r\n<p>where,<br />\r\n<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta\\)</span> = angle made by the liquid with the wetted tube wall.<br />\r\n<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\)</span> = specific weight of the liquid<br />\r\n<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\)</span> = surface tension<br />\r\nd= diameter of the capillary tube<br />\r\nh = height of the liquid in the vertical tube</p>",
    "important": "Surface Tension: \\[\\sigma = \\dfrac{F}{L}\\]\r\n\r\nCapillary rise: \\[h = \\dfrac{4\\sigma \\cos \\beta}{\\gamma d}\\]",
    "resources": "<div class=\"mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 flex flex-wrap gap-2\"><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1nDVkTo2LLPvHqo21c3f9Q8li57eZYKDK/preview\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"px-3 py-1.5 bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-900/40 text-purple-700 dark:text-purple-300 rounded text-xs font-bold hover:bg-purple-200 transition-colors\">👨‍🏫 Tutoring</a></div>",
    "images": [
      "https://onedrive.live.com/embed?resid=9C913AE725AFDEB1%21581&authkey=%21AOQoVTXCapx5-0Y&width=323&height=292"
    ],
