    "discipline": "Environmental",
    "subject": "Fluid Mechanics and Hydraulics",
    "chapter": "Fluid Statics",
    "topic": "Pascal's Law",
    "subtopic": "Pascal's Law",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"notes-image-container my-4 text-center flex flex-col items-center\"><center><img src=\"https://onedrive.live.com/embed?resid=9C913AE725AFDEB1%21506&authkey=%21AFzPxTNtqVFpi5g&width=340\" width=\"340\"/></center>\r\n<p><small><small>Credits: Source code contributed by Arne Timperman on StackExchange</small></small></p></div><p>Pascal's law or the principle of transmission of fluid-pressure is a principle in fluid mechanics that states that <b>pressure exerted anywhere in a confined incompressible fluid is transmitted equally in all directions throughout the fluid such that the pressure variations (initial differences) remain the same.</b></p>\r\n\r\n\r\nSince pressure is same everywhere in the fluid,\r\n\\begin{align*}\r\nP_1 &= P_2\\\\\r\n\\dfrac{F_1}{A_1} &= \\dfrac{F_2}{A_2}\\\\\r\n\\dfrac{A_2}{A_1} &= \\dfrac{F_2}{F_1}\\\\\r\n\\end{align*}\r\n<p>Since $A_2$ is kept far larger than $A_1$, efforts are magnified and we can lift loads that are too heavy to be lifted by human hands. This concept is used in Hydraulic press.</p>",
    "important": "",
    "resources": "<div class=\"mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 flex flex-wrap gap-2\"><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1csGIxeD22ZvQKEL4CVgmVjcxCDR8mIXF/preview\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"px-3 py-1.5 bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-900/40 text-purple-700 dark:text-purple-300 rounded text-xs font-bold hover:bg-purple-200 transition-colors\">👨‍🏫 Tutoring</a></div>",
