    "chapter": "Impulse-Momentum Principle",
    "topic": "Momentum Equation",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"container-fluid\">\r\n\t<div class=\"row align-items-start\">\r\n\t\t<div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n<p>It is based on the second law of motion, i.e. net force acting in any direction is equal to rate of change of momentum in that direction.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nLinear impulse = change in linear momentum<br />\r\n<br />\r\nConsidering x direction only,<br />\r\nInitial linear momentum <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pm\\)</span> linear impulse = Final linear momentum<br />\r\n\r\n\\begin{align*} \r\n\\rho Q t v_{x_1} + F_x. t &= \\rho Q t v_{x_2}\\\\ \r\nF_x &= \\rho Q (v_{x_2} - v_{x_1})\r\n\\end{align*}\r\n\r\n\r\n\t\t</div>\r\n    \t\t<div class=\"col\">\r\n     <center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Nozzle_flow_case_1.svg\" alt=\"Nozzle flow\" width=\"340\"><br/>\r\n\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nozzle_flow_case_1.svg\">Olivier Cleynen</a>, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    \t\t</div>\r\n  \t</div>\r\n</div>",
